# #<-- Publish Date --># # 2000-11-29 # #<-- Technical Document Summary (TDS) --># # The script exploits a setuid binary by invoking the rcp command through a crafted argument that sets the process to root privileges. It leverages a missing setuid check on a temporary file opened for writing, allowing execution of arbitrary commands as the privileged user. The vulnerability resides in the rcp utility's handling of user-writable files without proper permission validation. The exploit writes a shell binary and then uses system calls to spawn a shell on the target host. This demonstrates local privilege escalation via a setuid binary flaw. # #<-- Layman's Document Summary (LDS) --># # The weakness is that a file opened for writing by a non-root process can be executed as root if it has the SUID bit set, without proper permission checks. The exploit allows an attacker to run commands with elevated privileges by exploiting this misconfiguration. It targets the RedHat 6.2 Linux system's rcp command implementation. # #<-- Key Words (KW) --># # RedHat 6.2, /usr/bin/rcp, SUID local privilege escalation, setuid binary, privilege escalation, temporary file, shell execution, command injection, RCP, root privileges, chown, sudo, vulnerability, exploit script # #<-- Related Terms --># # setuid, privilege escalation, local execution, RedHat, rcp, SUID bit, temporary file, shell, command line, vulnerability classification # #<-- Entities/Attributes --># # Software: rcp, /usr/bin/rcp; Vulnerability: missing setuid check; Version: Not specified in source; OS: Linux; Architecture: Not specified in source; Programming language: Perl; File paths: /tmp/shell.c, hey, geezer; Functions: setuid, setgid, exec, unlink; Ports: Not specified in source # #<-- Vulnerability Type --># # Not specified in source # #<-- Original Content --># #!/usr/bin/perl -w # # exploits suid privledges on rcp # Not really tested this but hey # works on redhat6.2 # not werk on freebsd4.1 stable # # bug discovered by # Andrew Griffiths # # Exploit written by tlabs # greetz to those that know me innit # # Please set your rcpfile # this can be found by doing # # ls -alF `which rcp` # # have a lot of fun $RCPFILE="/usr/bin/rcp" ; # configure above innit sub USAGE { print "$0
Written by Tlabs
