// #<-- Publish Date --># // 2001-02-13 // #<-- Technical Document Summary (TDS) --># // The exploit leverages a buffer overflow in Elm version 2.5 PL3 caused by improperly sized stack allocation when processing command-line arguments via the -f (Alternative-Folder) option. The vulnerability arises from writing beyond the allocated buffer into adjacent memory, allowing arbitrary code execution. Affected component: Elm alternative-folder functionality; affected software: Elm 2.5 PL3. The exploit constructs a payload that overwrites return addresses and injects shellcode to invoke system commands. No successful exploitation is demonstrated in the source. // #<-- Layman's Document Summary (LDS) --># // The weakness stems from an unchecked buffer size when copying data into a fixed-size array, enabling an attacker to write beyond allocated memory. This allows execution of malicious code by overwriting critical program state. The affected component is Elm's command-line parsing logic for the -f option on Linux systems. // #<-- Key Words (KW) --># // elm 2.5, Alternative-Folder, buffer overflow, stack overflow, shellcode injection, system command execution, Linux, NOP sled, RET address, strcpy, strcat, strlen // #<-- Related Terms --># // buffer overflow, alternative-folder, command-line argument parsing, ELF, vulnerability, exploitation, shellcode, return address overwrite, Linux binary, OSVDB-85836 // #<-- Entities/Attributes --># // Software: Elm 2.5 PL3 // Version: 2.5 PL3 // Author: _kiss_ // Platform: Linux // Functions: dame_sp, main, strcpy, strcat, putenv, system // Command: /usr/local/bin/elm // Option: -f (Alternative-Folder) // CVE: Not specified in source // #<-- Vulnerability Type --># // Buffer overflow // Not specified in source // #<-- Original Content --># // source: https://www.securityfocus.com/bid/2403/info There is a buffer overflow in elm 2.5 PL3. This overflow is accessible by passing a long string to the -f (Alternative-Folder) command-line option