// #<-- Publish Date --># // 2003-10-27 // #<-- Technical Document Summary (TDS) --># // The program lacks bounds checking when passing user-supplied input to the sprintf() function, creating multiple buffer overrun vulnerabilities. This allows an attacker with sufficient privileges to execute arbitrary code on the affected system. The affected component is musicqueue.cgi in Musicqueue version 0.9/1.0/1.1. No successful exploitation is demonstrated in the source. <<< // #<-- Layman's Document Summary (LDS) --># // The weakness involves insufficient input validation leading to buffer overflows when handling user-supplied data. An attacker can execute arbitrary code with the privileges of the application, potentially as a root or suid user. The vulnerability resides in the local file handling mechanism of Musicqueue. No specific exploit success is confirmed. <<< // #<-- Key Words (KW) --># // Musicqueue, buffer overflow, sprintf, input validation, privilege escalation, local root exploit, CVE-2003-1140, OSVDB-2735 <<< // #<-- Related Terms --># // buffer overrun, arbitrary code execution, suid, local root, input sanitization, C library vulnerability <<< // #<-- Entities/Attributes --># // Musicqueue, version 0.9, version 1.0, version 1.1, Linux, sprintf, musicqueue.conf, execle, argv, file descriptor <<< // #<-- Vulnerability Type --># // Buffer overflow <<< // #<-- Original Content --># // source: https://www.securityfocus.com/bid/8903/info Multiple buffer overrun vulnerabilities have been discovered in Musicqueue. Both issues stem from the lack of bounds checking when passing user-supplied input to the sprintf() libc function. As a result, it may be possible for an attacker to exploit arbitrary code with the privileges the affected application, possibly installed suid or sgid. /* ** ** 0x82-musicqueue_over - musicqueue.cgi local root `Proof of Concept' exploit ** ** This is general overflow exploit. ** ** -- ** bash-2.04$ ./0x82-musicqueue_over /tmp/musicqueue-1.1.1