// #<-- Publish Date --># // 2005-01-26 // #<-- Technical Document Summary (TDS) --># // The program reads a hexadecimal string from command-line input and writes it to an executable. It then executes the supplied address as a binary payload. The vulnerability is a local privilege escalation where the user’s root session can be exploited by crafting a crafted input that triggers a segmentation fault in the target process. The affected component is the /usr/bin/trn utility on Mandrake and Slackware systems. // #<-- Layman's Document Summary (LDS) --># // The weakness lies in improper handling of user‑supplied data during execution, allowing an attacker to influence how memory is accessed when the program writes its payload. This leads to a privilege escalation without requiring additional system privileges. The exploit allows an attacker to cause the local root session to execute arbitrary code with elevated rights. // #<-- Key Words (KW) --># // trn, local privilege escalation, segmentation fault, command injection, vulnerability, Mandrake, Slackware, /usr/bin/trn, hexadecimal string, payload execution, root session, CVE not specified // #<-- Related Terms --># // exploit, local privilege escalation, segmentation fault, binary payload, command line argument parsing, Unix exploit, software vulnerability, privilege escalation technique // #<-- Entities/Attributes --># // Software: /usr/bin/trn // Versions: Mandrake 9.2, Slackware 10.0.0, Slackware 9.1.0 // Vendors: Mandrake, Slackware // Operating System: Linux // Architecture: Not specified // Programming Language: C // Protocols: Not applicable // File Paths: /etc/shadow, /trn-test.txt // Functions: strtoul, atoi, memset, sprintf, execl // Ports: Not applicable // CVE identifiers: Not specified in source // #<-- Vulnerability Type --># // Not specified in source // #<-- Original Content --># /* /usr/bin/trn local root exploit By ZzagorR - http://www.rootbinbash.com */ /* sh-2.05b$ ./trn usage : ./trn ret buf example